==English==
{{Webster 1913}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|matrone}}, {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|matrona||married woman}}, from {{m|la|mater||mother}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈmeɪtɹən/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|eɪtɹən|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A mature woman; a wife or a widow, especially, one who has borne children; a woman of staid or motherly manners.
#* {{w|William Shakespeare}} (c.1564–1616)
#*: your wives, your daughters, your '''matrons''', and your maids
#* {{w|Thomas Fuller}} (1606-1661)
#*: grave from her cradle, insomuch that she was a '''matron''' before she was a mother
#* {{RQ:Chmbrs YngrSt|IX}}
#*: “A tight little craft,” was Austin’s invariable comment on the '''matron'''; and she looked it, always trim and trig and smooth of surface like a converted yacht cleared for action. &para; Near her wandered her husband, orientally bland, invariably affable,{{nb...}}.
# A housekeeper; especially, a woman who manages the domestic economy of a public institution; a head nurse in a hospital.
#: {{ux|en|the '''matron''' of a school or hospital}}

